  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The specimen fits this scenario mainly by keeping transport and envelope semantics narrow: it names a handler, keeps payload bytes opaque, and says unsupported best-effort output is unverified. That supports the pressure to avoid inventing session meaning. But it is not a UDP-feed binding spec, it under-specifies totally non-decodable datagrams, and unsigned v0 weakens PromiseGrid-style audit and accountability.",
    "strengths": [
      "Very clear boundary between dispatch identity and opaque payload bytes.",
      "Unknown-handler output must stay marked unsupported and unverified.",
      "Local rejection or unsupported handling does not require a central registry or global state.",
      "Small DAG-CBOR positional array is straightforward to implement when decoding succeeds."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No explicit peer-local rule for bytes that fail envelope decoding before any dispatch decision.",
      "Base envelope provides no signature, so integrity and authorship are pushed to other layers.",
      "Promise accounting and long-lived audit evidence are mostly outside this specimen."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Best-effort salvage could become de facto message interpretation if tooling or operators over-trust it.",
      "Malformed or injected unsigned traffic may be hard to distinguish during later audits."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What exact raw-byte record should Bob keep for a datagram that is not valid DAG-CBOR at all?",
      "Should the UDP binding require pass-through or logging of original datagram bytes independent of envelope parsing?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
